WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.626 --> 00:00:05.588
Let's say I want to drive from Vox's 
New York office to our office in DC.

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:10.427
That produces about 
112 kilograms of CO2.

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:13.263
But in an electric vehicle or EV

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:16.349
that same drive produces 
zero emissions.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.935
It's all thanks to this 
rechargeable battery

00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:22.355
a lithium ion battery that 
powers the car's electric motor.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.566
If all road vehicles were electric

00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:28.820
we'd be able to wipe out about 
18% of all CO2 emissions

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.239
the most prominent greenhouse gas 
driving climate change.

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:36.995
But producing these powerful batteries 
can do real environmental damage.

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:39.581
Nickel, the key ingredient 
in EV batteries

00:00:39.581 --> 00:00:41.458
has to be mined and smelted

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:44.127
in a process that contaminates 
waterways

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:46.796
drives deforestation and 
pollutes the air.

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:49.090
And there's no better place to see this

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.093
than outside nickel processing plants 
in Indonesia 

00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:54.554
next to where people 
like Asvina live.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.523
These particles are finer 
than beach sand

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:08.151
and are harmful when inhaled. 

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:10.862
The same nickel paving the way 
for a green future

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:12.739
is causing life threatening problems 

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.492
for the people who live near 
its production centers.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.535
But it shouldn't be this way 

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:20.705
because Indonesia is sitting on 
one of the biggest sources 

00:01:20.705 --> 00:01:23.249
of clean energy in the world.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.960
An energy source that could help 
clean up the dirty 

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.755
and often hidden side of 
green energy.

00:01:33.468 --> 00:01:36.304
Indonesia has the world's largest 
proven nickel reserves.

00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:38.848
Most of them are found here.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:42.602
So is a large concentration of the country's
nickel processing plants.

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:46.815
A lot of this nickel supplies the steel industry,
but most of the growth

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:51.361
the industry has seen in recent years is 
driven by the demand for EV batteries.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.947
Demand that's predicted to skyrocket.

00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:55.782
To extrack the nickel

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:58.409
the rocks have to be smelted 
at really high heats.

00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:02.122
And that energy is almost exclusively provided by

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:06.042
coal fired plants that spew greenhouse gases 
and pollute the air.

00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:19.472
Asvina's story isn't an isolated incident.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.142
Records of medical visits 
in her area show that

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:25.478
respiratory infections are by far 
the most common health issue.

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:28.356
Data from 2017 and 2018

00:02:28.356 --> 00:02:31.568
suggests respiratory diseases are 
increasing in the region

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:34.070
at a much faster rate 
than other ailments.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.656
But the government has yet 
to release the latest data.

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:40.994
For Asvina, who has lived in the area 
since she was a child

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.413
there's no doubt things are worsening.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.023
Nickel is essential for a green future

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:13.818
but using coal fired plants 
isn't actually necessary

00:03:13.818 --> 00:03:16.237
especially in Indonesia.

00:03:16.237 --> 00:03:19.240
Indonesia sits along the Pacific Ocean's
Ring of Fire

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.828
a stretch of hundreds of active volcanoes
that sit on top of pools of hot magma.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:29.292
We only really see the immense power of this heat
when it pierces through the earth's surface.

00:03:29.626 --> 00:03:31.628
But when it's close to the surface

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:34.714
that magma also heats the water 
trapped beneath the earth.

00:03:35.256 --> 00:03:36.633
That hot water can provide 

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:39.302
That hot water can provide a continuous 
and renewable flow of energy 

00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:41.179
called geothermal energy.

00:03:41.846 --> 00:03:43.181
To capture that energy

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:45.892
we need to drill down to reach
underground water.

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:49.187
Then hot water or steam rise up to a well.

00:03:49.187 --> 00:03:53.274
In a power plant, that hot water is often used 
to heat a different liquid

00:03:53.274 --> 00:03:57.445
that is then vaporized and used to turn a turbine
to generate electricity.

00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:00.698
Meanwhile, the clean water extracted is funneled

00:04:00.698 --> 00:04:04.285
back into the ground where the earth's magma 
heats it once again.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.747
And that fluid is recycled.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:12.585
So there are no emissions of any gases 
to the atmosphere.

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.797
In that sense, it's a completely green

00:04:15.797 --> 00:04:17.632
carbon-free energy source.

00:04:17.632 --> 00:04:21.261
Plus, it doesn't rely on the weather
like wind or solar energy do.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:25.598
Indonesia is the second largest 
geothermal producer in the world.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:29.394
On the same island where coal-fired plants 
are powering nickel production

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:31.729
there's a plant tapping into 
geothermal power.

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:35.108
There are about 20 active
geothermal plants.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:39.028
There are also tens of sites
explored for development.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:42.448
One of the biggest things holding 
geothermal back in Indonesia

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.368
and other parts of the world 
is cost.

00:04:45.493 --> 00:04:47.870
And then once you've got evidence that there's a resource

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:50.873
the idea is then to figure out  
how big is the resource

00:04:50.873 --> 00:04:52.625
how hot is the resource

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.378
and how much would it cost to 
develop that type of resource?

00:04:55.378 --> 00:04:57.463
Longer timeline, higher risk factor

00:04:57.463 --> 00:05:00.466
and higher initial investment costs

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:05.263
are all things that make geothermal
more challenging to put online.

00:05:05.513 --> 00:05:09.559
And while geothermal maps like this one 
can help identify possible hotspots...

00:05:10.059 --> 00:05:13.354
you never know what you're going to find 
until you actually drill.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.189
Over time, the hope is that 

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:18.901
geothermal exploration will become cheaper,
more predictable

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:21.404
and so efficient that it'll bring the cost down.

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:25.658
But it can be tough to change 
an existing industry,

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.910
especially if there's a lot of money in it.

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:31.581
Encouraged by Indonesia's 
push to attract foreign investment

00:05:31.581 --> 00:05:34.167
and deregulation of environmental 
protections

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.377
Chinese companies have 
invested or committed 

00:05:36.377 --> 00:05:42.133
about $30 billion to nickel plants 
in Indonesia, particularly in Morawali

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:44.177
where new coal fired plants like this one

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:45.970
being built to power 
the investment.

00:05:46.721 --> 00:05:48.014
For people like Asvina

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:51.351
the fact that geothermal doesn't produce 
emissions or air pollution

00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:53.311
could make it the solution 
they're looking for.

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:55.730
Because if nothing changes

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:57.523
they might have to 
leave their homes.

00:06:09.494 --> 00:06:13.539
Today, geothermal plants are mostly 
confined to volcanic areas

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:16.292
but our EV batteries are made of 
metals and minerals

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:18.044
from around the world.

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:21.422
And about 60% of the energy
we used to process them

00:06:21.422 --> 00:06:23.257
comes from fossil fuels.

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:26.677
There's enormous potential for cleaner 
EV battery production

00:06:26.677 --> 00:06:29.931
in all these yellow and red regions 
if we dig deeper

00:06:29.931 --> 00:06:32.809
and find ways to tap into the underground heat

00:06:32.809 --> 00:06:34.936
whether there's underground water or not.

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:38.439
Like every new resource, 
the work we do  to harness it

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:40.400
requires careful consideration.

00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:42.735
How do you preserve parklands

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.695
and how does that coexist with 
geothermal development?

00:06:44.779 --> 00:06:46.447
The other issue that seems
to come up a lot

00:06:46.447 --> 00:06:48.908
when I read about geothermal is
seismic activity. 

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:55.748
Most of the geothermal induced seismicity
that occurs is very low level seismicity

00:06:55.748 --> 00:07:00.086
but the goal is to not have significant seismicity
that could cause damage

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:03.381
and distress to local communities.

00:07:03.631 --> 00:07:05.508
The challenges are to making these 

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:08.761
environmentally, socially, 
and economically viable.

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:11.013
And that's a very important challenge

00:07:11.013 --> 00:07:14.100
especially if we think of 
geothermal as a solution 

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.186
to clean up the supply chain 
that powers our clean energy.

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:19.188
Because all too often 

00:07:19.188 --> 00:07:23.234
it's poor and marginalized communities
who live next to power plants

00:07:23.234 --> 00:07:27.321
smelters, mines, factories, 
pipelines, waste plants.

00:07:27.738 --> 00:07:30.116
As we move towards a better future

00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:34.203
it's important to make sure 
it isn't just green but fair.

